udns 0.5: fix the inet_pton()/inet_ntop() configure probe prototypes

`configure` decides whether the C library provides inet_pton()/inet_ntop()
by compiling and linking a probe program.  In 0.5 that probe passes a
`long *` where inet_pton() expects a `const char *` and returns the
`const char *` of inet_ntop() from an `int` main(), so it is ill-formed:

    int main() {
      char buf[64];
      long x = 0;
      inet_pton(AF_INET, &x, buf);
      return inet_ntop(AF_INET, &x, buf, sizeof(buf));
    }

clang rejects this outright ("incompatible pointer to integer conversion
returning (const char *) from a function with result type (int)" is a hard
error, not a downgradable warning), so the probe fails and configure
writes NO `#define HAVE_INET_PTON_NTOP 1` into config.h.

The consequence is a silently broken library: udns_XtoX.c only defines
the public `dns_ntop()`/`dns_pton()` entry points inside the
`#ifdef HAVE_INET_PTON_NTOP` branch.  Without the define it falls back to

    #define inet_XtoX_prefix udns_
    #include "inet_XtoX.c"

which builds `udns_ntop()`/`udns_pton()` instead, although udns.h still
declares `dns_ntop()`/`dns_pton()`.  libudns.a then ships a symbol set
that does not match its own header and every consumer fails to link:

    ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: dns_pton
    >>> referenced by test.c
    >>> referenced by udns_resolver.c: udns_resolver.o:(dns_add_serv)
    ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: dns_ntop

Upstream fixed the probe in 0.6, which is why the same recipe builds and
links cleanly for udns 0.6 but not for 0.5.

Fix: use the corrected probe from upstream 0.6, whose argument and return
types match the C library declarations.  With the probe passing,
configure defines HAVE_INET_PTON_NTOP and udns_XtoX.c provides the
declared `dns_ntop()`/`dns_pton()` wrappers.

--- a/configure	2026-09-30 18:42:58.581579510 +0800
+++ b/configure	2026-09-30 18:42:58.603579518 +0800
@@ -94,8 +94,9 @@
 int main() {
   char buf[64];
   long x = 0;
-  inet_pton(AF_INET, &x, buf);
-  return inet_ntop(AF_INET, &x, buf, sizeof(buf));
+  inet_ntop(AF_INET, &x, buf, sizeof(buf));
+  inet_pton(AF_INET, buf, &x);
+  return x;
 } 
